exercises.instructions:
The `try/catch` statement lets you handle errors without crashing your program. Code in the `try` block runs normally. If an error occurs, execution jumps to the `catch` block.

```
try {
  // risky code
} catch (error) {
  // handle the error
}
```

Write a function called `safeParse` that takes a string `jsonStr` and tries to parse it with `JSON.parse()`. If parsing succeeds, return the parsed value. If it fails, return the string `"Invalid JSON"`.

Example: `safeParse('{"a":1}')` should return `{ a: 1 }`
Example: `safeParse('not json')` should return `"Invalid JSON"`

exercises.solution:
function safeParse(jsonStr) {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(jsonStr);
  } catch (error) {
    return "Invalid JSON";
  }
}

exercises.testRunner:
(code) => {
  const fn = new Function(code + '; return safeParse;')();
  const r1 = fn('{"a":1}');
  const r2 = fn('not json');
  const r3 = fn('"hello"');
  return [
    { pass: r1 && r1.a === 1, description: 'safeParse(\'{"a":1}\') returns { a: 1 }', got: JSON.stringify(r1) },
    { pass: r2 === 'Invalid JSON', description: 'safeParse("not json") returns "Invalid JSON"', got: String(r2) },
    { pass: r3 === 'hello', description: 'safeParse(\'"hello"\') returns "hello"', got: String(r3) },
    { pass: code.includes('try') && code.includes('catch'), description: 'Uses try/catch', got: code.includes('try') ? 'Yes' : 'No' }
  ];
}

