exercises.instructions:
When a `while` loop should always execute at least once, `do...while` is the better choice. It moves the condition check to the end.

The function `reverseNumber(n)` reverses the digits of a non-negative integer. Here is the `while` loop version — notice it fails for `n = 0` because the condition is false from the start:

```
function reverseNumber(n) {
  let reversed = 0;
  while (n > 0) {
    reversed = reversed * 10 + n % 10;
    n = Math.floor(n / 10);
  }
  return reversed;
}
// reverseNumber(0) returns 0... but only by accident (reversed starts at 0)
```

Rewrite this function using a `do...while` loop to properly handle `n = 0`. Your solution must use `do...while` — not `while`.

Example: `reverseNumber(123)` returns `321`.

Example: `reverseNumber(100)` returns `1`.

exercises.starterCode:
// Rewrite using do...while to guarantee at least one iteration.
// The body runs first, then the condition is checked.

// Must use do...while — not while
function reverseNumber(n) {

}

exercises.solution:
function reverseNumber(n) {
  let reversed = 0;
  do {
    reversed = reversed * 10 + n % 10;
    n = Math.floor(n / 10);
  } while (n > 0);
  return reversed;
}

